"<thinking>\nStep A: Text Breakdown:\n1. \"We are currently developing a mobile football app that will be available for Android and iOS devices.\"\n2. \"...the app runs quickly and smoothly so that it is fully loaded and ready to use within a maximum of two seconds after starting.\"\n3. \"...offer football fans a central platform where they can stay comprehensively informed at all times.\"\n4. \"...users can display current football news of their favorite teams and also select preferred sports channels individually.\"\n5. \"In addition to the news, users can follow the results of their favorite teams live via a live ticker.\"\n6. \"Our app will offer extensive coverage of all leagues and competitions, both national and international.\"\n7. \"Detailed team and player information will also be available at any time.\"\n8. \"Integration of live stream links, for example, from providers such as DAZN or Sky Sport.\"\n9. \"These [live streams] are available directly in the app as soon as the live broadcast has begun.\"\n10. \"Compliance with transmission rights is maintained. Live streams will only be displayed when the respective rights are fulfilled in the country.\"\n11. \"Option to retrieve the live results of their favorite teams in real time.\"\n12. \"The app includes an integrated live ticker module that enables the retrieval and display of live data in real time.\"\n13. \"The user interface is designed so that users can access the information in the app directly.\"\n14. \"The live ticker module communicates directly via the interface with the user interface to display and continuously update current match data.\"\n15. \"The live data is retrieved via an API from an external server. The server is located outside the app and provides the data dynamically.\"\n16. \"In addition to the live ticker module, the app also offers additional modules that allow users to individually configure the app and connect their own interface to the user interface.\"\n17. \"The app is linked to a notification system that provides users with information about current events as soon as the notification function is activated.\"\n18. \"All modules and the user interface are fixed components of the app and remain consistent even with external server updates.\"\n19. \"Registration and login are designed to be uncomplicated and can be completed directly at app startup.\"\n20. \"Users have the option to personalize their experience by following favorite clubs and receiving notifications about news or results of their favorite teams.\"\n21. \"Users can share news and game reports via social media directly from the app.\"\n22. \"App...functions reliably even with limited network coverage.\"\n23. \"The app remains usable offline, even if it temporarily loses connection.\"\n24. \"On match days with a particularly high number of accesses, our app is designed so that up to 100,000 users can be served simultaneously without performance loss.\"\n25. \"App goes through a thorough testing and support process before release to detect and resolve any malfunctions early.\"\n26. \"User feedback and app reviews are continuously evaluated to improve functionality.\"\n27. \"All personal data of users are processed in compliance with the GDPR.\"\n\nStep B: Requirement Extraction & Sorting (Initial FR/NFR Assignment):\nFrom the above breakdown:\n- FR Candidates:\n  1. Android and iOS support (1)\n  2. Show news for favorite teams, select teams and sports channels (4, 20)\n  3. Live ticker for favorite teams in real time (5, 11, 12, 14)\n  4. Coverage of all leagues and competitions (6)\n  5. Show detailed team/player info (7)\n  6. Show live streams via links (8, 9)\n  7. Hide/display live streams based on transmission rights and country (10)\n  8. News/event push notifications (17)\n  9. Registration/login at app startup, simple (19)\n  10. Social sharing of news/game reports (21)\n  11. App modules allow configuration/connect external interface (16)\n\n- NFR Candidates:\n  1. Runs quickly/smoothly, load within 2 seconds (2)\n  2. Reliable with poor network/offline mode (22, 23)\n  3. Scale for 100,000 concurrent users without performance loss (24)\n  4. App and modules stable through external server updates (18)\n  5. Testing/release and support process (25)\n  6. Continuous user feedback evaluation (26)\n  7. GDPR-compliant personal data processing (27)\n\nStep C: De-duplication & Overlap Check:\n- (4) and (20) are similar — cover user news/feed can be personalized by team/channel selection, and notification by favorite team.\n- (5), (6), (7) — showing available info, ensure details (team/player, all leagues) — overlap as 'extensive, up-to-date info and coverage provided,' can be kept as distinct per stakeholder, but may be consolidated.\n- (8), (17) — notifications for current events, covered under push notification, but (20) also speaks to notifications for news/results — consolidate into one notification FR, be specific.\n- (22), (23) — offline and unreliable networks go together.\n- (25), (26) — Testing (pre-release quality) and feedback-driven optimization are distinct.\n- (10) Live stream only shown if rights in country; ensure that's a system behavior.\n- (16) ‘Modules connect own interface’ is vague. Likely an extension/configurability feature. State as ability to configure modules/user interface.\n\nStep D: BDD Planning:\n- User persona: Football fan (likely mobile, possibly traveling, wants to follow favorite team, get news/results, customize feed, watch live, etc.)\n- For each requirement:\n    - News/feed selection: Given user on feed screen, When selecting teams/channels, Then news updates filtered.\n    - Live ticker: Given watching a match, When favorite team playing, Then see live ticker updates in real time.\n    - League/competition/team/player coverage: Given user searches for competition/team/player, When action taken, Then info available.\n    - Live stream: Given match is live and user’s region is eligible, Then live stream link displayed (else hidden).\n    - Notifications: Given user enables notifications, When event occurs (goal, news, etc.), Then notification received.\n    - Registration: Given launch/startup, When user does registration/login, Then process is direct and successful.\n    - Social sharing: Given viewing news/report, When tapping share, Then platform options appear and content is posted.\n    - App configuration: Given settings, When user adjusts modules/interface, Then changes take effect.\n    - Android/iOS: Given device type, App installs/works.\n    - Performance: Given app launch, Within 2s must see dashboard. With 100,000 users, system remains responsive.\n    - Offline/reliability: Given loss of connection, Can still use app/core features.\n    - Testing: Pre-release process/test coverage.\n    - Feedback: Given feedback/review, When submitted, Then appears in backlog/considered.\n    - GDPR: Given personal data collection, Then only in compliance mode.\n\nStep E: ID Assignment:\nAssigning sequential IDs based on above decomposition — FR-001 through etc. for functionals (will be 11), NFR-001 through etc. for non-functionals (will be 7).\n\nFinal plan: Functional Reqs ~11; Non-Functional ~7. Each will pair with the corresponding user story and BDD acceptance, with Source Reference mapped to the relevant text fragment above.\n</thinking>"